Sure! To get your `myItemList` sorted alphabetically (ascending) with all items that have a status of `"urgent"` moved to the top, you can use the `prioritizeAndSort` helper.  
Just feed it your array of objects and it will return the sorted result.

```javascript
// Example usage
const myItemList = [
  { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Bravo", status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Delta", status: "urgent" }
];

// Call the helper
const sorted = prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),   // pass the array as a JSON string
  priorityStatus: "urgent",
  ascending: "true"                    // ascending order for non‑urgent items
});

console.log(sorted);
// Output:
// [
//   { name: "Bravo", status: "urgent" },
//   { name: "Delta", status: "urgent" },
//   { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
//   { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" }
// ]
```

If you already have your `myItemList` defined, just replace the example array above with your own data. Let me know if you’d like me to run this on your actual list!
